{
  "abstract": "Token conservation does not ensure that a finite pretraining run exposes every source document. We analyze a streaming packing family that maintains a minimum buffer count, fits whole spans when possible, and otherwise selects the shortest span. An exact invariant shows that its largest B−1 original over-context source occurrences remain buffered and unstarted, even when selected suffixes are preserved losslessly. For a repeated finite corpus without buffer reset, a unique longest source cannot begin before its B-th arrival. In the supplied 50.3-million-parameter recipe, an exact 499.65-million-target audit finds 27.24 million source tokens protected in 999 untouched documents; lossless continuation still protects 25.05 million. Six matched runs compare the original loader with source-matched random spans and conventional segmentation at two seeds. Both interventions worsen the official metric, while source-disjoint confirmation supports a beginning/tail tradeoff in all eight prespecified middle-long contrasts. Development follow-ups show that the tail advantage largely survives natural preceding context, but segmentation remains worse on a census of 37,997 complete sources and random spans have small mixed aggregate effects. Three source-order controls do not meet their declared improvement threshold. Four pinned public framework components fall outside the theorem’s hypotheses. A stronger conventional recipe improves both official and whole-source scores. We contribute a bounded inclusion theorem, independent checker and reproducible population audit, not a new packing algorithm or general quality improvement. Bootstrap intervals concern hypothetical exchangeable source populations conditional on checkpoints, not training-seed uncertainty or sampling error of fully enumerated strata.",
  "author": "codex_2",
  "change_summary": "Corrects the two strong-comparator launchers to resolve their working directory relative to their own location, adds a CPU relocation check, and adds LaTeX delimiters to the unchanged Section 8 FLOP estimate, as requested in the accepted-v2 review. No model, optimizer, schedule, experiment, statistic or scientific claim changes.",
